.Dd July 27, 2026
.Dt SECUREARC 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm securearc
.Nd ARC chain validation and sealing milter for Postfix
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl c Ar config-file
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a high-performance Authenticated Received Chain (ARC) milter implementing
RFC 8617.
It validates existing ARC chains on incoming mail and optionally seals
messages transiting through the mail system, preserving authentication
results across forwarding hops.
.Pp
.Nm
must be placed last in the milter chain (after SPF, DKIM, and DMARC)
so that all upstream Authentication-Results are available for inclusion
in the ARC-Authentication-Results header.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl c Ar config-file
Path to the INI-style configuration file.
Required.
.El
.Sh CONFIGURATION
The configuration file uses INI format with a
.Ql [global]
section and one or more
.Ql [listener:name]
sections.
.Ss Global Section
.Bl -tag -width "SyslogFacility" -compact
.It Cm AuthservID
Identifier for Authentication-Results headers.
Default:
.Dq localhost .
.It Cm LocalAuthMethods
Comma-separated list of authentication methods this ADMD evaluates locally,
for example
.Dq spf, dkim, dmarc .
When sealing, only Authentication-Results headers that claim our
.Cm AuthservID
and assert nothing outside this list are copied into the
ARC-Authentication-Results header and signed.
A host that lists no local methods has authenticated nothing, so its AAR
reports
.Dq none
rather than repeating results supplied by the sender
(RFC 8617 Section 5.1.1).
Default: empty.
.It Cm StripAuthResults
Remove every pre-existing Authentication-Results header claiming our
.Cm AuthservID ,
not just the ones asserting an
.Li arc=
result.
Enable this when
.Nm
is the first or only milter in the chain (RFC 8601 Section 5).
Default: no.
.It Cm Mode
Default operating mode for any
.Ql [listener:name]
section that does not set its own
.Cm Mode :
.Dq seal ,
.Dq verify ,
or
.Dq both .
Falls back to
.Dq verify
if neither this nor a per-listener
.Cm Mode
is set.
See
.Cm Mode
under
.Sx Listener Sections
for the per-listener override.
.It Cm WorkerThreads
Number of worker threads.
0 means auto-detect CPU count.
Default: 0.
.It Cm MaxConnections
Maximum simultaneous connections per worker.
Default: 256.
.It Cm PidFile
Path to the PID file.
Default:
.Pa /var/run/securearc/securearc.pid .
.It Cm Foreground
Run in foreground without daemonizing.
Default: no.
.It Cm User
Drop privileges to this user, written as
.Ql user
or
.Ql user:group .
The drop happens before the listeners are bound, so a unix-domain socket is
created owned by this user and group, and the directory holding it must be
writable by them.
Naming a group is what allows the socket to be shared with the MTA and nothing
else; see
.Cm UMask .
Refused if the name resolves to UID 0, which would be a privilege drop that
drops nothing.
.It Cm UMask
File-creation mask for the PID file and any unix-domain listener, in octal.
Unset inherits the mask the daemon was started with, which leaves a unix socket
at 0755 and so reachable by every local account \(em an unauthenticated sealing
oracle for anyone with a shell on the host.
.Li 0117
creates it at 0660, owner and group only.
A socket takes its mode from the umask in force when it is bound and cannot be
narrowed afterwards without a window at the wider mode, so this is the only
control over its permissions.
Has no effect on a TCP listener, which has no filesystem permissions.
Read as octal whether or not a leading zero is written; a value that is not
valid octal is an error rather than a silent fallback.
.It Cm Syslog
Enable syslog output.
Default: yes.
.It Cm SyslogFacility
Syslog facility.
Default: mail.
.It Cm LogLevel
Logging verbosity: err, warn, info, debug.
Default: info.
.It Cm DnsNameserver
Comma-separated DNS nameserver IPs.
Default:
.Dq 127.0.0.1 .
.It Cm DnsTimeout
DNS query timeout in seconds.
Default: 5.
.It Cm DnsRetries
Number of DNS query retries.
Default: 2.
.It Cm DnsCacheSize
Maximum DNS cache entries per worker.
Default: 1000.
.It Cm DnsNegativeTTL
Negative cache TTL in seconds.
Default: 60.
.It Cm SignedHeaders
Colon-separated headers included in ARC-Message-Signature.
Default:
.Dq from:to:subject:date:message-id .
.It Cm MaxBodyBytes
Largest message body buffered in order to hash it.
Accepts an optional
.Cm K , Cm M
or
.Cm G
suffix.
Default: 10M.
0 disables the limit.
.Pp
Should be at least the MTA's
.Va message_size_limit .
A body over the limit is neither hashed nor silently truncated: chain validation
reports
.Cm arc=temperror
and sealing is skipped, since an
.Cm ARC-Message-Signature
taken over a partial copy would name this ADMD as the hop that broke the chain.
.It Cm MaxHeaders
Largest number of headers accumulated for one message.
Default: 500.
0 disables the limit.
.It Cm MaxHeaderBytes
Largest total header size, names plus values, accumulated for one message.
Accepts a size suffix.
Default: 1M.
0 disables the limit.
.Pp
A message exceeding either header limit is tempfailed rather than delivered.
Headers past the limit are never read, and
.Nm
can only strip a forged
.Cm Authentication-Results
header that it read.
.Pp
.Cm MaxHeaders
is also what bounds ARC verification cost.
One ARC set is three headers and RFC 8617 permits 50 sets, so a full-length
chain is 150 headers by itself; do not tune this below roughly 300 where deep
chains are expected.
There is no
.Cm MaxSignatures
for
.Nm :
RFC 8617 Section 5.1.1 already caps a chain at 50 sets and
.Nm
fails any chain longer than that, so rejecting a shorter, RFC-legal chain to
save work would be a conformance bug rather than hardening.
.Pp
.Cm MaxBodyBytes , Cm MaxHeaders
and
.Cm MaxHeaderBytes
are read at startup only.
.Dv SIGHUP
does not change them.
.It Cm MinimumKeyBits
Smallest RSA key, in bits, accepted on an
.Cm ARC-Message-Signature
or
.Cm ARC-Seal .
Default: 1024.
.Pp
ARC inherits DKIM's cryptography, so RFC 8301 \[sc]3.2 applies to sealed hops as
well: verifiers
.Dq MUST NOT consider signatures using RSA keys of less than 1024 bits as valid signatures .
Unlike the
.Cm Max Ns -prefixed
limits above, 0 does not disable this check; a configured value below 1024 is
raised to 1024 and a warning is logged.
.Pp
A set signed with an undersized key fails the chain
.Pq Cm arc=fail ,
because a hop whose signature proves nothing cannot be a link in a chain of
custody.
The reported reason names the key rather than the signature, so the failure can
be taken up with the forwarder that produced it instead of being mistaken for
message modification in transit.
.Pp
This option governs only keys published by other ADMDs.
The seal key configured with
.Cm SealKeyFile
is always held to the 1024-bit floor irrespective of this setting, and the daemon
refuses to start if it is smaller.
Read at startup only.
.It Cm MaxKeyRecords
How many key records to try at one selector before giving up.
Default: 3.
Values above 8 are clamped to 8.
.Pp
A domain rotating keys publishes the old and the new record at the same
selector for a time, and RFC 6376 \[sc]6.1.2 leaves their order unspecified
\(em resolvers commonly rotate it.
Trying only the first would make the verdict depend on which half DNS happened
to return, so the same message could validate on one lookup and fail on the
next.
.Pp
This matters more for ARC than for DKIM.
A failed
.Cm ARC-Message-Signature
or
.Cm ARC-Seal
makes this hop write
.Cm cv=fail ,
and RFC 8617 \[sc]5.2.1 makes that permanent for the life of the message: every
later hop reads it and none may revise it.
A transient publishing state would become a permanent chain break.
.Pp
The first record that verifies wins, so the ordinary single-key case costs
exactly one public-key verification and a rotation costs one extra only when the
record listed first is the wrong half of the pair.
.It Cm MaxEvaluationMs
The wall-clock ceiling in milliseconds for evaluating one message, shared by
every DNS lookup the verdict needs.
Default: 20000.
0 disables it.
.Pp
Exhausting the budget yields temperror, never fail: the message was not
judged, and the sender retries.
A slow resolver, not a hostile sender, is what it guards against.
.It Cm On-DNSError
What a sealing listener does when a previous hop's key cannot be fetched
because DNS failed transiently
.Pq SERVFAIL, timeout, unreachable resolver .
One of
.Dq tempfail ,
.Dq skip-seal
or
.Dq seal-fail .
Default:
.Dq tempfail .
An unrecognised value is refused at startup.
.Pp
RFC 8617 Section 5.2.1 makes
.Cm cv=fail
permanent for the life of a message: no later hop may revise it.
A transient nameserver failure is therefore the one condition where the
cheapest local response is the most destructive one, because the cost lands
entirely on the sender and every downstream receiver rather than on this host.
.Bl -tag -width "skip-seal" -compact
.It Dq tempfail
Defer with a 4xx and let the sender retry.
The only non-destructive answer for a hop that modifies messages, which is what
sealing exists for.
The risk is a lame delegation that keeps returning a transient-looking error:
the message then occupies the queue until
.Cm maximal_queue_lifetime
expires and bounces.
.It Dq skip-seal
Add no ARC set, report
.Cm arc=temperror
and pass the message through unchanged.
No delay.
Correct for a relay that does not alter messages.
Note that it does
.Em not
rescue the chain if this hop does alter them: the previous hop's
.Cm ARC-Message-Signature
then covers content that changed, the next hop computes
.Cm cv=fail ,
and the effect is only to move which hop breaks the chain.
Whether this path rewrites mail is something the operator knows and the daemon
cannot, which is why this is configuration rather than a heuristic.
.It Dq seal-fail
Seal
.Cm cv=fail .
The behaviour of releases before this option existed.
Not recommended: it is the only setting that permanently destroys a chain which
may be perfectly valid.
.El
.Pp
Verifying listeners are not configurable and always report
.Cm arc=temperror
and deliver.
A verifier records no permanent verdict in the message, so it has no such
decision to make.
An internal fault on this host \(em an allocation failure, for instance \(em
always defers, in either role, and is never charged to the sender's chain.
.It Cm ZmqEndpoint
ZeroMQ PUB endpoint for event publishing.
Disabled if not set.
.It Cm ZmqTopic
ZeroMQ topic prefix.
Default:
.Dq arc .
.El
.Ss Listener Sections
Each
.Ql [listener:name]
section defines a socket and its operating mode.
.Bl -tag -width "SealSelector" -compact
.It Cm Socket
Listen address.
Format:
.Dq inet:port@ip
or
.Dq unix:/path .
The
.Ar ip
must be a numeric IPv4 or IPv6 address; no name resolution is performed.
A value that cannot be parsed is a fatal configuration error and is reported
with the section that contains it \(em it is never ignored, and never falls
back to the default listener.
.It Cm Mode
Operating mode for
.Em this listener :
.Dq seal ,
.Dq verify ,
or
.Dq both .
A listener that does not set
.Cm Mode
inherits
.Cm Mode
from the
.Ql [global]
section, or
.Dq verify
if that is unset.
Any other value is a configuration error and
.Nm
refuses to start.
.Pp
.Dq both
validates the chain and then extends it in a single pass: one chain
evaluation feeds both the
.Li arc=
stamp and the new set's
.Li cv= ,
so the two can never disagree, and the stamp is included in the set's
ARC-Authentication-Results (a split verify+seal deployment pays two
validations and its AAR completeness depends on MTA header delivery between
the listeners).
This is the recommended mode for a relay hop that both checks and extends
chains.
.Pp
Each listener is independent.
Check the
.Ql listener[N] mode=...
lines logged at startup against the intended configuration, particularly when
a verify section and a seal section both appear as in the sample
configuration.
.It Cm SealDomain
Domain name for ARC sealing.
Required for seal mode.
.It Cm SealSelector
DNS selector for the ARC signing key.
Required for seal mode.
.It Cm SealKeyFile
Path to the private key file for ARC sealing.
Required for seal mode.
Supports RSA and Ed25519 keys in PEM format.
.El
.Pp
.Cm SealDomain ,
.Cm SealSelector
and
.Cm SealKeyFile
are accepted in a listener section but apply to the whole daemon, not to that
listener alone: there is one seal key.
Per-listener sealing identities are not supported.
.Pp
If no listener sections are defined, the default is
.Dq inet:8895@127.0.0.1
in verify mode, loopback only.
.Pp
The milter protocol carries no authentication, so any client that can reach a
listener is trusted completely.
On a
.Cm seal
listener this is an unauthenticated sealing oracle: the caller obtains an ARC set
signed under
.Cm SealDomain
and
.Cm SealSelector .
On a
.Cm verify
listener the caller dictates the
.Va Authentication-Results
field this host records, which a subsequent seal then vouches for.
Only the local MTA should ever connect.
Bind a routable address only when the MTA runs on a different machine, and
restrict access to it with
.Xr pf 4 .
.Sh SIGNALS
.Bl -tag -width "SIGTERM" -compact
.It Dv SIGHUP
Reload configuration.
The configuration file is re-read and the reloadable settings are adopted as a
single unit, so a message is always processed entirely under one configuration:
.Va AuthservID ,
.Va SealDomain ,
.Va SealSelector ,
.Va SignedHeaders ,
.Va LocalAuthMethods ,
.Va StripAuthResults ,
.Va MinimumKeyBits ,
.Va MaxKeyRecords ,
.Va On-DNSError ,
the seal key file, and the resolver settings
.Pq Va Nameservers , Va DNSTimeout , Va DNSRetries , Va DNSCacheSize , Va DNSNegativeTTL .
.Pp
If the file cannot be read or parsed, or the new settings cannot be applied, the
previous configuration stays in effect and the reason is logged; a reload is
never applied by halves.
.Pp
The following are read at startup only, because a running process cannot change
them honestly.
.Va Listen
and the per-listener
.Va Mode
are bound to open sockets, and a mode cannot change under a connection that has
already been accepted.
.Va MaxBodyBytes ,
.Va MaxHeaders
and
.Va MaxHeaderBytes
are fixed when a worker is spawned.
.Va ZmqEndpoint
and
.Va ZmqTopic
are fixed because each worker holds its own publisher socket for the lifetime of
the process.
.Va WorkerThreads ,
.Va PidFile ,
.Va User
and
.Va Foreground
are startup decisions.
Changing any of these requires a restart.
.It Dv SIGTERM
Graceful shutdown with 30 second drain timeout.
.El
.Sh POSTFIX INTEGRATION
.Nm
must be last in the milter chain:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
smtpd_milters = inet:127.0.0.1:8890,
                inet:127.0.0.1:8891,
                inet:127.0.0.1:8894,
                inet:127.0.0.1:8895
milter_connect_macros = j {daemon_name} v {client_addr}
milter_default_action = accept
.Ed
.Sh LOG FORMAT
.Bd -literal -offset indent
securearc[pid]: id=QUEUEID peer=HOST[IP] client=IP listener=N mode=verify|seal|both elapsed=Nms
.Ed
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/usr/local/etc/securearc/securearc.conf"
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/securearc/securearc.conf
Default configuration file.
.It Pa /var/run/securearc/securearc.pid
Default PID file.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr securearc-check 8 ,
.Xr securearc-seal 8 ,
.Xr securearc-testkey 8 ,
.Xr securespf 8 ,
.Xr securedkim 8 ,
.Xr securedmarc 8
.Sh STANDARDS
.Rs
.%T Authenticated Received Chain (ARC) Protocol
.%R RFC 8617
.Re
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Daniel Morante
